The focus here will be on Out-Of-Band (OOB) access.
Basically OOB access is the art of controlling a server without using its normal network or OS.

The objective here is to teach 'Infr Survivability'

Normal way (In-Band);
Laptop → SSH → Server OS → do work
If SSH breaks, one is locked out of the server. This is called In-Band access because the same "band" (Path) is used , just like normal traffic.

Out-Of-Band (OOB):
Laptop → Console/Rescue/Serial → Direct machine control
Notice here that ; SSH, firewall, network,sudo, services are all bypassed (....talk directly to the machine).
Keyboard plugged into the server, that's OOB access because a seperate control path is used.
Out-Of_band access types:
 *Vm console (VirtualBox/VMware/Promax)
 *Cloud serial console (AWS ec2 serial console/Azure serial console/GCP console)
 *Rescue/Recovery Mode : OOB access allows repair of server when normal access (SSH/network/OS) is broken, and Resue/Recovery mode is linux's built-in environment for repair work(s).
It allow direct machine control.

Out-Of_band finds application in the following sceanrios;
When SSH or OS is unusable.

Common real world failures-

*lost sudo
*broken sudoers
*SSH misconfig
*firewall lock-out
*wrong network config
*disk full
*bad fstab (won't boot)
*permission chaos
*kernel boot failure

Out-of-Band access is an alternative management path that lets you control a server without relying on its normal OS or network. 
Recovery mode is Linux’s minimal boot environment that provides a root shell for repairing issues like broken SSH, sudo, or boot failures.

Every serious linux otage follows ; Boot - Root - Repair









